// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 14): space.
ADDART({id:"euclidroman",t:"The Euclid and Roman Space Telescopes",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔭",w:"Euclid (spacecraft)",r:["darkmatter","jwst","hubblest","lagrange","lensing"],
p:[["Mapping the dark universe","Euclid and Roman map billions of galaxies to study dark matter and dark energy."],["Euclid launched in 2023","Europe’s Euclid released its first big catalog in November 2026."],["Roman launched in 2026","NASA’s Roman telescope launched in August 2026."]],
l:"The Euclid and Nancy Grace Roman space telescopes are wide-field observatories designed to map huge areas of the sky and help solve two of the biggest mysteries in science: dark matter and dark energy, which together make up about 95 percent of the universe. Euclid, a European Space Agency mission with NASA participation, launched on a SpaceX Falcon 9 on 1 July 2023 and observes from near the L2 Lagrange point, 1.5 million kilometers from Earth. Its 1.2-meter mirror and two instruments, a visible-light camera and an infrared spectrometer, are surveying about a third of the sky over six years, measuring the shapes and distances of billions of galaxies. Tiny distortions in galaxy shapes caused by gravitational lensing reveal how dark matter is spread out, while the pattern of galaxies over time shows how dark energy has driven the universe’s expansion. On 12 November 2026, Euclid released a catalog of more than 900 million galaxies. NASA’s Roman Space Telescope, named after astronomer Nancy Grace Roman, launched on a Falcon Heavy on 30 August 2026. With a 2.4-meter mirror like Hubble’s but a field of view at least 100 times larger, it will also search for planets and study dark energy, with first images expected in early 2027.",
s:[["Euclid’s first images","Euclid’s first color images, released in November 2023, showed galaxy clusters, nebulae and star clusters in remarkable detail across wide areas. Its wide field captures in one shot an area larger than the full Moon."],["Dark energy","Astronomers discovered in 1998 that the universe’s expansion is speeding up, driven by an unknown dark energy. By measuring the 3D positions of galaxies and how lensing changes over cosmic time, Euclid and Roman will test whether dark energy has stayed constant."],["Roman’s planet search","Roman will watch the center of the Milky Way for microlensing, brief brightenings when a star and its planets pass in front of a more distant star. Astronomers expect it to find thousands of planets, including cold worlds and free-floating planets with no star."],["Nancy Grace Roman","Nancy Grace Roman was NASA’s first chief of astronomy, starting in 1959, and championed the Hubble Space Telescope, earning the nickname ‘mother of Hubble’. She died in 2018."]],
k:[["Euclid launched","1 July 2023 (ESA)"],["Euclid mirror","1.2 meters"],["Roman launched","30 August 2026 (NASA)"],["Roman mirror","2.4 meters"],["Location","Near the Sun–Earth L2 point"]],
d:[["1998","Accelerating expansion of the universe discovered"],["2023","Euclid launched"],["2023","Euclid’s first images released"],["2026","Roman Space Telescope launched"],["2026","Euclid releases a catalog of 900 million galaxies"]],
s2:[["Data floods","Euclid’s first major release contained about 1.7 petabytes of data, and Roman is expected to send about 1.4 terabytes a day, more than any earlier NASA astrophysics mission. Astronomers use AI to classify galaxies and find rare objects."],["Working together","Euclid, Roman and the Vera C. Rubin Observatory on the ground observe overlapping areas of sky, and combining their data will give a sharper picture of the universe’s structure."]],
sp:["Roman ahead of schedule","Roman was completed under budget and launched months earlier than its official deadline of May 2027, an unusual achievement for a large space telescope."],
g:[["Dark energy","The unknown cause of the universe’s accelerating expansion"],["Gravitational lensing","Bending of light by gravity"],["Field of view","How much sky a telescope sees at once"],["Microlensing","Brightening of a star by a passing object’s gravity"]],
f:["Euclid launched in 2023.","Roman launched in August 2026.","Euclid’s first catalog listed over 900 million galaxies."],
fun:"Roman has the same size mirror as Hubble but can see at least 100 times more sky at once."});

ADDART({id:"eht",t:"The Event Horizon Telescope",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🕳️",w:"Event Horizon Telescope",r:["bh","sagittariusa","radioastronomy","relat","galaxies","m87bh"],
p:[["An Earth-sized telescope","The EHT links radio dishes around the world."],["First picture of a black hole","In 2019 it showed the black hole in galaxy M87."],["Our own black hole","In 2022 it imaged Sagittarius A* at the Milky Way’s center."]],
l:"The Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) is a worldwide network of radio telescopes that work together as a single, Earth-sized telescope to photograph the regions around black holes. Black holes themselves give off no light, but the hot gas swirling around them glows brightly, and the black hole casts a dark ‘shadow’ against it. Because black holes are so far away, seeing this shadow requires extremely sharp vision, equivalent to reading a newspaper in New York from a café in Paris. The EHT achieves this using a technique called very-long-baseline interferometry: radio dishes in places such as Hawaii, Chile, Spain, Antarctica, Mexico and Arizona observe the same target at the same time, record data with atomic clocks and combine it later with supercomputers. On 10 April 2019, the EHT collaboration released the first image of a black hole, a glowing ring around the giant black hole at the center of the galaxy M87, about 55 million light-years away and 6.5 billion times the mass of the Sun. On 12 May 2022, it released an image of Sagittarius A*, the black hole at the center of our Milky Way. The images confirmed predictions of Einstein’s general theory of relativity.",
s:[["Very-long-baseline interferometry","A telescope’s sharpness depends on its size. By combining dishes thousands of kilometers apart, the EHT works like a telescope as wide as Earth. Each site records data on hard drives, so much data that the drives were flown to processing centers rather than sent over the internet."],["The M87 image","The M87 black hole’s shadow is about the size of our solar system. The image showed a bright ring brighter on one side, because gas moving toward Earth appears brighter. Later images showed the magnetic fields around it."],["Sagittarius A*","Sagittarius A* is much smaller and closer than M87’s black hole, and gas around it changes within minutes, making it harder to image, like photographing a running child. Its ring looked strikingly similar to M87’s, despite being about 1,500 times less massive."],["Team effort","More than 300 researchers from many countries worked on the first images. Computer scientist Katie Bouman, who helped develop imaging methods, became widely known after a photo of her reaction to the first image went viral."]],
k:[["Type","Global network of radio telescopes"],["Technique","Very-long-baseline interferometry"],["First black hole image","M87*, released 10 April 2019"],["Milky Way black hole image","Sagittarius A*, released 12 May 2022"],["M87* mass","About 6.5 billion Suns"]],
d:[["2009","Event Horizon Telescope project begins"],["2017","Global observations of M87 and Sagittarius A*"],["2019","First image of a black hole released"],["2021","Image of magnetic fields around M87’s black hole"],["2022","Image of Sagittarius A* released"]],
s2:[["Antarctic dish","The South Pole Telescope, in one of the driest and coldest places on Earth, is part of the EHT. Dry air lets the short radio waves the EHT uses pass through with little absorption."],["Next steps","Astronomers plan to add more telescopes, including possibly dishes in space, to make sharper images and even movies of gas swirling around black holes."]],
sp:["Too much data for the internet","The 2017 observations produced about 5 petabytes of data on half a ton of hard drives. Data from the South Pole had to wait months for the end of the Antarctic winter to be flown out."],
g:[["Event horizon","The boundary beyond which nothing can escape a black hole"],["Interferometry","Combining signals from several telescopes"],["Shadow","The dark region a black hole creates against glowing gas"],["Petabyte","About a million gigabytes"]],
f:["The EHT links telescopes around the world.","The first black hole image was released in 2019.","Our galaxy’s black hole was imaged in 2022."],
fun:"The EHT’s data was so large that it was flown around the world on hard drives instead of being sent online."});

ADDART({id:"seti",t:"SETI: The Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"👽",w:"Search for extraterrestrial intelligence",r:["fermiparadox","astrobio","radioastronomy","exoplanet","habitablezone"],
p:[["Listening for aliens","SETI scientists search for signals from intelligent life beyond Earth."],["Project Ozma","Frank Drake began the first modern search in 1960."],["The Wow! signal","A mysterious signal in 1977 was never detected again."]],
l:"SETI, the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence, is the scientific effort to detect evidence of technological civilizations beyond Earth, mainly by listening for radio or light signals that could not be produced naturally. In 1959, physicists Giuseppe Cocconi and Philip Morrison suggested that radio telescopes could detect alien broadcasts. In 1960, astronomer Frank Drake carried out Project Ozma, pointing a radio telescope at Green Bank, West Virginia, at two nearby Sun-like stars, Tau Ceti and Epsilon Eridani. In 1961, he wrote the Drake equation, which estimates the number of communicating civilizations in our galaxy by multiplying factors such as the rate of star formation and the fraction of planets that develop life. On 15 August 1977, the Big Ear radio telescope in Ohio detected a strong, narrow signal that so impressed astronomer Jerry Ehman that he wrote ‘Wow!’ on the printout; it was never detected again, and its origin remains unknown. The SETI Institute was founded in California in 1984, and the Breakthrough Listen project, launched in 2015, is the largest search yet. No confirmed signal has been found, but the discovery of thousands of exoplanets has made the search more focused.",
s:[["How the search works","SETI researchers look for signals with features nature does not make, such as extremely narrow radio frequencies or very short laser pulses. Computers sift billions of channels, and most candidate signals turn out to be interference from satellites, phones or other human sources."],["SETI@home","From 1999 to 2020, the SETI@home project let millions of volunteers donate spare computer time to analyze radio telescope data, one of the first large volunteer computing projects."],["Technosignatures","Besides radio signals, scientists look for technosignatures such as laser flashes, unusual dimming of stars by giant structures or industrial pollution in exoplanet atmospheres."],["Sending messages","In 1974, the Arecibo radio telescope beamed a short coded message toward a star cluster about 25,000 light-years away. Some scientists debate whether sending messages, called active SETI, is wise."]],
k:[["First modern search","Project Ozma, 1960"],["Drake equation","1961"],["Wow! signal","15 August 1977"],["SETI Institute founded","1984"],["Largest search","Breakthrough Listen (2015)"]],
d:[["1959","Cocconi and Morrison propose radio searches"],["1960","Project Ozma"],["1961","Drake equation presented"],["1974","Arecibo message sent"],["1977","Wow! signal detected"],["1984","SETI Institute founded"],["2015","Breakthrough Listen launched"]],
s2:[["Contact","Carl Sagan’s 1985 novel Contact, made into a 1997 film starring Jodie Foster, told the story of a SETI scientist who detects an alien signal. The character was partly inspired by astronomer Jill Tarter."],["The Allen Telescope Array","The Allen Telescope Array in northern California, partly funded by Microsoft co-founder Paul Allen, is a group of radio dishes built for SETI and other astronomy."]],
sp:["Little green men","When Jocelyn Bell Burnell discovered regularly pulsing radio signals in 1967, the source was jokingly labeled LGM-1, for ‘little green men’. It turned out to be a pulsar, a spinning neutron star."],
g:[["SETI","Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence"],["Technosignature","Evidence of technology from another civilization"],["Drake equation","A formula estimating the number of alien civilizations"],["Interference","Unwanted signals from human sources"]],
f:["Project Ozma began in 1960.","The Wow! signal was detected in 1977.","No confirmed alien signal has been found."],
fun:"The astronomer who found the 1977 Wow! signal circled it on the printout and wrote ‘Wow!’ next to it."});

ADDART({id:"marsorbiters",t:"Mars Orbiters",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter",r:["mars","marsrovers","marinerprogram","hopeprobe","tianwen"],
p:[["Eyes above Mars","Orbiters circle Mars, mapping it and relaying rover messages."],["A crowded orbit","Spacecraft from the US, Europe, India, the UAE and China have orbited Mars."],["Lost and found","Some orbiters have failed, but others have lasted decades."]],
l:"Mars orbiters are spacecraft that circle Mars to study its surface, atmosphere and climate and to relay data from landers and rovers to Earth. NASA’s Mariner 9 became the first spacecraft to orbit another planet in 1971. The Soviet Union and NASA’s Viking orbiters followed in the 1970s. NASA’s Mars Global Surveyor, operating from 1997 to 2006, found gullies suggesting recent water flows. Mars Odyssey, which arrived in 2001, detected large amounts of buried ice and became the longest-serving spacecraft at Mars. The European Space Agency’s Mars Express, in orbit since 2003, found evidence of ancient water and possible underground lakes of salty water under the south polar ice cap. NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter, arriving in 2006, carries HiRISE, a camera that can see objects about a meter across, and has photographed rovers and landers on the surface. MAVEN, which arrived in 2014, showed how the solar wind stripped away much of Mars’s atmosphere; NASA lost contact with it in December 2025 and ended the mission in 2026. India, the United Arab Emirates and China have also placed orbiters around Mars. Not all missions succeed: NASA’s Mars Climate Orbiter was lost in 1999 because of a mix-up between metric and imperial units.",
s:[["Relaying messages","Rovers such as Curiosity and Perseverance send most of their data to orbiters passing overhead, which relay it to Earth faster than the rovers could transmit directly. Orbiters are therefore vital for surface missions."],["Watching Mars change","Orbiters have seen avalanches, new impact craters, dust storms, seasonal frost and shifting sand dunes, showing that Mars is still an active world."],["Mars Climate Orbiter","In 1999, one team used pound-force units while another expected metric newtons. The error sent the spacecraft too deep into Mars’s atmosphere, where it was destroyed, a famous lesson in checking units."],["International orbiters","India’s Mars Orbiter Mission arrived in 2014, the ESA–Russian ExoMars Trace Gas Orbiter in 2016 and the UAE’s Hope and China’s Tianwen-1 in February 2021, making Mars orbit an international neighborhood."]],
k:[["First Mars orbiter","Mariner 9, 1971"],["Long-serving orbiter","Mars Odyssey (since 2001)"],["Mars Express","ESA, since 2003"],["Sharpest camera","HiRISE on Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter"],["Lost in 1999","Mars Climate Orbiter (units mix-up)"]],
d:[["1971","Mariner 9 enters Mars orbit"],["1997","Mars Global Surveyor arrives"],["1999","Mars Climate Orbiter lost"],["2001","Mars Odyssey arrives"],["2003","Mars Express arrives"],["2006","Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter arrives"],["2014","MAVEN and India’s Mangalyaan arrive"],["2025","Contact with MAVEN lost"]],
s2:[["Underground lakes","In 2018, scientists using Mars Express radar reported a possible lake of salty liquid water about 1.5 kilometers beneath Mars’s south polar ice. Some later studies suggested the bright signals could have other causes, and the debate continues."],["Where did the air go?","MAVEN measured gas escaping from Mars’s upper atmosphere and showed that the solar wind removed much of the planet’s once-thicker atmosphere over billions of years, helping explain how Mars dried out."]],
sp:["Spotting landers","HiRISE photographed NASA’s Phoenix lander descending under its parachute in 2008 and Perseverance’s parachute in 2021, capturing spacecraft landing on another planet from orbit."],
g:[["Orbiter","A spacecraft that circles a planet"],["Relay","Passing data from one spacecraft to another"],["HiRISE","A powerful camera on the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter"],["Atmospheric escape","Gas leaking from a planet into space"]],
f:["Mariner 9 was the first Mars orbiter.","Mars Odyssey has served at Mars since 2001.","A units mix-up destroyed Mars Climate Orbiter."],
fun:"A mix-up between pounds and newtons caused NASA to lose the Mars Climate Orbiter in 1999."});

ADDART({id:"lucymission",t:"Lucy: Visiting the Trojan Asteroids",c:"Space Exploration",i:"💎",w:"Lucy (spacecraft)",r:["asteroidbelt","jupiter","psyche","solarform","orbits"],
p:[["Fossils of the solar system","Lucy is the first mission to Jupiter’s Trojan asteroids."],["A record number of targets","It will visit more asteroids than any spacecraft before."],["Named after a fossil","Lucy is named after a famous human ancestor fossil."]],
l:"Lucy is a NASA spacecraft launched on 16 October 2021 to explore the Trojan asteroids, two swarms of rocky bodies that share Jupiter’s orbit around the Sun, one leading the planet and one trailing it. The Trojans are thought to be leftovers from the formation of the outer planets and may hold clues to how the solar system formed, which is why the mission is named after Lucy, the 3.2-million-year-old fossil skeleton of a human ancestor that taught scientists about human evolution. The fossil, in turn, was named after the Beatles song ‘Lucy in the Sky with Diamonds’. Lucy uses repeated gravity assists from Earth to travel on a long, looping path. On 1 November 2023, it flew past the small main-belt asteroid Dinkinesh and discovered that it has a moon, later named Selam, which itself turned out to be two rocks touching. On 20 April 2025, Lucy flew past the asteroid Donaldjohanson, named after the fossil’s discoverer. It is scheduled to reach its first Trojan, Eurybates, in 2027 and to finish with the double Trojan Patroclus and Menoetius in 2033, visiting more asteroids than any earlier mission. Its two huge solar arrays are each about 7 meters across.",
s:[["The Trojans","The Trojan asteroids gather around two balance points in Jupiter’s orbit, called the L4 and L5 Lagrange points, 60 degrees ahead of and behind the planet. Many are named after heroes of the Trojan War from Greek legend."],["Solar array trouble","After launch, one of Lucy’s two fan-like solar arrays did not fully latch open. Engineers spent months carefully running its motors to pull it nearly into place, allowing the mission to continue."],["Dinkinesh and Selam","Dinkinesh, named after the Ethiopian name for the Lucy fossil, meaning ‘you are marvelous’, was a test target. The discovery that its moon Selam is a contact binary surprised scientists."],["Gravity assists","Lucy flew past Earth in 2022 and 2024, using Earth’s gravity to boost it toward the Trojans. Its complex path makes the most of its fuel to reach targets on both sides of Jupiter."]],
k:[["Launched","16 October 2021"],["Destination","Jupiter’s Trojan asteroids"],["First flyby","Dinkinesh, 1 November 2023"],["First Trojan","Eurybates, 2027"],["Final targets","Patroclus and Menoetius, 2033"]],
d:[["2021","Lucy launched"],["2022","First Earth gravity assist"],["2023","Dinkinesh flyby; moon Selam discovered"],["2024","Second Earth gravity assist"],["2025","Donaldjohanson flyby"],["2027","Planned arrival at Eurybates"]],
s2:[["The Lucy fossil","The Lucy fossil, an Australopithecus afarensis discovered in Ethiopia in 1974 by Donald Johanson and colleagues, showed that human ancestors walked upright before they developed large brains."],["A message for the future","Lucy carries a plaque with messages from famous people, including Beatles members, and a diagram of the planets’ positions at launch, for any future visitors."]],
sp:["Diamonds in the sky","Lucy’s instruments include one with a diamond beam splitter, a fitting nod to the Beatles song ‘Lucy in the Sky with Diamonds’."],
g:[["Trojan asteroid","An asteroid sharing a planet’s orbit"],["Lagrange point","A balance point in an orbit"],["Contact binary","Two bodies touching each other"],["Flyby","Passing close to an object without stopping"]],
f:["Lucy launched in 2021.","It is the first mission to Jupiter’s Trojans.","It is named after a famous fossil."],
fun:"The Lucy spacecraft is named after a fossil, which was named after a Beatles song."});

ADDART({id:"juice",t:"JUICE: Exploring Jupiter’s Icy Moons",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🧊",w:"Jupiter Icy Moons Explorer",r:["ganymede","europa","jupiter","europaclipper","galileoprobe"],
p:[["Europe to Jupiter","JUICE is ESA’s mission to Jupiter and its icy moons."],["Hidden oceans","It will study oceans that may lie beneath the moons’ ice."],["Orbiting Ganymede","It will be the first spacecraft to orbit a moon of another planet."]],
l:"JUICE, the Jupiter Icy Moons Explorer, is a European Space Agency mission to study Jupiter and three of its large moons, Ganymede, Callisto and Europa, which are thought to have oceans of liquid water beneath their icy surfaces. It launched on an Ariane 5 rocket from French Guiana on 14 April 2023, one of the last Ariane 5 flights. To reach Jupiter, JUICE uses a series of gravity assists: in August 2024 it made the first-ever combined flyby of the Moon and Earth, and it flew past Venus in August 2025, with further Earth flybys to follow. It is scheduled to arrive at Jupiter in July 2031. After dozens of flybys of the moons, JUICE is planned to enter orbit around Ganymede in December 2034, becoming the first spacecraft to orbit a moon other than Earth’s Moon. Ganymede, the largest moon in the solar system, is bigger than the planet Mercury and the only moon known to have its own magnetic field. JUICE carries cameras, radar that can probe kilometers into the ice, spectrometers and magnetometers. Its huge solar panels, covering about 85 square meters, are needed because sunlight at Jupiter is about 25 times weaker than at Earth.",
s:[["Ganymede","Ganymede’s ocean may lie about 150 kilometers beneath its icy crust and may contain more water than all of Earth’s oceans. JUICE will study the ice shell, the ocean and Ganymede’s magnetic field, which produces auroras."],["Callisto and Europa","Callisto’s ancient, heavily cratered surface preserves a record of the early solar system. JUICE will make two flybys of Europa, complementing NASA’s Europa Clipper, which is focused on that moon."],["A stuck antenna","After launch, JUICE’s 16-meter radar antenna was stuck in its folded position. Engineers fired a device to shake it free, and after several attempts, the antenna fully deployed in May 2023."],["Surviving radiation","Jupiter’s powerful magnetic field traps intense radiation that can damage electronics. JUICE’s most sensitive electronics are shielded in vaults lined with lead and other metals."]],
k:[["Agency","European Space Agency"],["Launched","14 April 2023 (Ariane 5)"],["Arrival at Jupiter","July 2031 (planned)"],["Ganymede orbit","December 2034 (planned)"],["Solar panel area","About 85 square meters"]],
d:[["2012","ESA selects JUICE"],["2023","JUICE launched"],["2023","Radar antenna freed"],["2024","First combined Moon–Earth flyby"],["2025","Venus flyby"],["2031","Planned arrival at Jupiter"]],
s2:[["Why icy moons?","Liquid water, energy and chemical ingredients are essential for life as we know it. Moons with hidden oceans are among the best places in the solar system to look for environments where life could exist."],["Juice and Clipper","Europa Clipper, launched in 2024, will arrive at Jupiter in 2030, before JUICE. The two missions will share data and may observe Jupiter at the same time."]],
sp:["Lunar–Earth flyby","In August 2024, JUICE flew past the Moon and then, about a day later, past Earth, a never-before-tried maneuver that saved fuel and helped steer it toward Venus."],
g:[["Icy moon","A moon with a surface of ice"],["Subsurface ocean","A body of liquid water beneath a surface"],["Magnetometer","An instrument that measures magnetic fields"],["Gravity assist","Using a planet’s gravity to change speed"]],
f:["JUICE launched in 2023.","It will reach Jupiter in 2031.","It will orbit Ganymede, the largest moon."],
fun:"JUICE will be the first spacecraft ever to orbit a moon of another planet, Ganymede, which is bigger than Mercury."});

ADDART({id:"dragonflytitan",t:"Dragonfly: A Drone for Titan",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚁",w:"Dragonfly (Titan space probe)",r:["titan","saturn","ingenuity","cassini","astrobio"],
p:[["Flying on Titan","Dragonfly is a NASA drone that will fly across Saturn’s moon Titan."],["Thick air, low gravity","Titan’s atmosphere makes flying easier than on Earth."],["Launch in 2028","It is planned to launch in 2028 and arrive in 2034."]],
l:"Dragonfly is a NASA mission to send a car-sized, nuclear-powered rotorcraft to Titan, Saturn’s largest moon. Titan is the only moon with a thick atmosphere and the only place besides Earth known to have liquid on its surface, in the form of lakes and seas of methane and ethane. Its atmosphere is about four times denser than Earth’s at the surface, and its gravity is about one-seventh of Earth’s, so flying is much easier there than on Earth. Dragonfly, selected by NASA on 27 June 2019 and built by the Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory, has eight rotors and will fly from place to place, landing to study the surface. Planned to launch in July 2028 on a SpaceX Falcon Heavy and arrive in 2034, it will explore sand dunes of organic material near the equator and travel toward Selk crater, where liquid water from an ancient impact may have mixed with organic compounds, the kind of chemistry that may have led to life on Earth. Powered by a radioisotope generator, it will make flights of up to several kilometers, eventually covering more than 100 kilometers in total. It follows the European Huygens probe, which landed on Titan in 2005.",
s:[["Why Titan?","Titan’s atmosphere is mostly nitrogen, like Earth’s, and sunlight turns methane into complex organic molecules that rain down onto the surface. Scientists think Titan resembles a frozen version of the early Earth, offering a natural laboratory for prebiotic chemistry."],["Flying on another world","Because radio signals take more than an hour to travel between Earth and Titan, Dragonfly must fly itself, choosing safe landing sites with its own cameras and sensors."],["Instruments","Dragonfly will drill into the surface and analyze samples with a mass spectrometer, measure weather, listen for seismic activity and take pictures. It will study whether Titan has chemistry similar to that which led to life."],["Huygens","The Huygens probe, carried by the Cassini spacecraft, parachuted to Titan’s surface on 14 January 2005, the most distant landing ever made. Its pictures showed rounded pebbles of ice, likely shaped by flowing liquid."]],
k:[["Selected","27 June 2019"],["Rotors","Eight"],["Planned launch","July 2028 (Falcon Heavy)"],["Planned arrival","2034"],["Power","Radioisotope generator"]],
d:[["2005","Huygens lands on Titan"],["2017","Cassini mission ends"],["2019","NASA selects Dragonfly"],["2024","Dragonfly confirmed for full development"],["2028","Planned launch"],["2034","Planned arrival at Titan"]],
s2:[["Methane seas","Titan’s largest sea, Kraken Mare, is larger than the Caspian Sea. Rain of liquid methane falls from clouds, carving rivers and filling lakes, in a cycle similar to Earth’s water cycle."],["Learning from Ingenuity","NASA’s Ingenuity helicopter, which flew on Mars from 2021 to 2024, proved that powered flight on another world is possible, giving engineers confidence in Dragonfly."]],
sp:["Wings on Titan","Titan’s air is so thick and its gravity so weak that a person wearing strapped-on wings could, in theory, fly by flapping their arms."],
g:[["Rotorcraft","An aircraft lifted by spinning rotors"],["Prebiotic chemistry","Chemical steps that may lead to life"],["Methane","A gas that is liquid at Titan’s temperatures"],["Radioisotope generator","A power source using heat from radioactive material"]],
f:["Dragonfly will fly on Titan.","Titan has lakes of liquid methane.","It is planned to launch in 2028."],
fun:"On Titan, a person with strapped-on wings could in theory fly by flapping their arms."});

ADDART({id:"marssamplereturn",t:"Mars Sample Return",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🧪",w:"Mars sample-return mission",r:["marsrovers","mars","tianwen","astrobio","osirisrex"],
p:[["Bringing Mars to Earth","Perseverance has collected rock samples meant to be returned to Earth."],["Too costly?","NASA’s original return plan became too expensive."],["China’s plan","China plans to bring back Martian samples around 2031."]],
l:"Mars sample return refers to efforts to bring rocks, soil and air from Mars to Earth, where scientists could study them with laboratory instruments far more powerful than any that can be sent to Mars, searching especially for signs of ancient life. NASA’s Perseverance rover, which landed in Jezero Crater on 18 February 2021, has been collecting samples in sealed tubes, more than 30 in all, from an ancient river delta and lake bed. In July 2024, it found a rock nicknamed ‘Cheyava Falls’ with leopard-spot patterns, and in September 2025, NASA announced that its sample, called Sapphire Canyon, contained features that could be a potential biosignature, though non-biological explanations remain possible. NASA and the European Space Agency planned a complex campaign involving a lander, a small rocket to launch the samples into Mars orbit and an orbiter to bring them home. However, estimates rose to about 11 billion dollars, with return not until 2040, and in January 2026 the US Congress declined to fund the existing program, leaving the samples on Mars for now. China’s Tianwen-3 mission plans to launch around 2028 and return samples by about 2031.",
s:[["Why bring samples home?","Earth laboratories can measure the ages, chemistry and microscopic structure of rocks with extreme precision. Samples can also be stored for decades and studied with instruments not yet invented, as happened with Apollo Moon rocks."],["Perseverance’s samples","Perseverance has sealed tubes containing rock cores, soil and even Martian air. In 2022–2023, it left ten tubes at a site called Three Forks as a backup collection that a future mission could pick up."],["The plan and its problems","The planned mission would have included the first launch of a rocket from another planet. An independent review in 2023 found the costs and schedule unrealistic, and NASA studied cheaper options before Congress stopped funding the existing program."],["Planetary protection","Samples returned from Mars would be handled in secure laboratories to protect Earth in case they contained life, and to keep the samples free of Earth contamination."]],
k:[["Collecting rover","Perseverance (landed 2021)"],["Samples","More than 30 sealed tubes"],["Estimated cost of NASA plan","About 11 billion dollars"],["Congress decision","January 2026: existing program not funded"],["China’s Tianwen-3","Return planned around 2031"]],
d:[["2021","Perseverance lands and collects its first rock core"],["2022","Sample depot begun at Three Forks"],["2023","Review criticizes the cost of Mars Sample Return"],["2024","Perseverance finds the ‘Cheyava Falls’ rock"],["2025","NASA reports a potential biosignature"],["2026","Congress declines to fund the existing program"]],
s2:[["Potential biosignature","A biosignature is a feature that may have been produced by life. NASA stressed that the Cheyava Falls features need more study, ideally in Earth laboratories, to rule out non-biological causes."],["Mars rocks already on Earth","About 400 meteorites found on Earth are thought to have come from Mars, blasted off by impacts. One, ALH84001, caused excitement in 1996 when scientists claimed it showed possible fossil bacteria, a claim most experts now doubt."]],
sp:["A rocket from Mars","No rocket has ever launched from the surface of another planet. A Mars ascent vehicle would need to be small, light and able to sit for months in Mars’s cold before firing."],
g:[["Sample return","Bringing material from space to Earth"],["Biosignature","A possible sign of life"],["Sample depot","A cache of samples left for later pickup"],["Ascent vehicle","A rocket that launches from a planet’s surface"]],
f:["Perseverance has sealed more than 30 samples.","NASA’s return plan was estimated at 11 billion dollars.","China plans to return Mars samples around 2031."],
fun:"Perseverance has even collected a tube of Martian air."});

ADDART({id:"ingenuity",t:"Ingenuity: The Mars Helicopter",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚁",w:"Ingenuity (helicopter)",r:["marsrovers","mars","helicopters","dragonflytitan","flight"],
p:[["First flight on another planet","Ingenuity made the first powered flight on another world in 2021."],["Tiny but tough","It weighed just 1.8 kilograms."],["72 flights","Planned for five flights, it made 72."]],
l:"Ingenuity was a small robotic helicopter that made the first powered, controlled flight on another planet. Weighing about 1.8 kilograms, it traveled to Mars attached to the belly of NASA’s Perseverance rover, which landed in Jezero Crater in February 2021. Flying on Mars is difficult because its atmosphere is only about 1 percent as dense as Earth’s, so Ingenuity’s two stacked rotors, each about 1.2 meters across, spun in opposite directions at about 2,400 revolutions per minute. On 19 April 2021, Ingenuity rose about 3 meters, hovered and landed, a flight lasting 39 seconds that NASA compared to the Wright brothers’ first flight. A small piece of fabric from the Wright Flyer was attached to it. Designed as a technology demonstration of up to five flights in 30 days, Ingenuity kept flying for almost three years, scouting routes for Perseverance and making 72 flights, traveling about 17 kilometers in total and reaching heights of 24 meters. On its 72nd flight, on 18 January 2024, its rotor blades were damaged during landing, and NASA ended the mission on 25 January 2024. It remains on Mars, still collecting weather data.",
s:[["Engineering challenges","Ingenuity had to survive nights colder than −80°C, kept warm by heaters powered by a small solar panel and batteries. It flew itself, because radio signals between Earth and Mars take several minutes, and used a downward camera to track its motion over the ground."],["Wright brothers’ field","NASA named the site of Ingenuity’s first flight Wright Brothers Field. The International Civil Aviation Organization gave it the official airfield code JZRO."],["Scouting","After its demonstration, Ingenuity became a scout, flying ahead of Perseverance to photograph terrain and help plan the rover’s route, showing how aircraft could help future exploration."],["The end","Ingenuity likely lost track of the smooth, featureless sand beneath it, causing a hard landing that damaged its blades. Images later showed one blade lying some distance away."]],
k:[["Mass","About 1.8 kilograms"],["First flight","19 April 2021"],["Rotor speed","About 2,400 rpm"],["Total flights","72"],["Mission ended","25 January 2024"]],
d:[["2020","Launched with Perseverance"],["2021","Lands on Mars attached to Perseverance"],["2021","First powered flight on another planet"],["2021","Begins scouting for Perseverance"],["2024","72nd and final flight"]],
s2:[["Smartphone parts","To save time and money, Ingenuity used off-the-shelf parts, including a processor similar to those in smartphones, showing that commercial electronics could survive in space."],["Future Mars aircraft","Ingenuity’s success inspired designs for larger Mars helicopters that could carry instruments and explore cliffs and caves rovers cannot reach."]],
sp:["A piece of history","A postage-stamp-sized piece of fabric from the wing of the Wright brothers’ 1903 Flyer was attached under Ingenuity’s solar panel, linking the first flights on two worlds."],
g:[["Helicopter","An aircraft lifted by rotors"],["Technology demonstration","A mission to test whether something works"],["Rotor","A spinning blade that lifts an aircraft"],["Scout","To explore ahead to gather information"]],
f:["Ingenuity first flew on Mars on 19 April 2021.","It weighed about 1.8 kilograms.","It made 72 flights."],
fun:"Ingenuity carried a tiny piece of fabric from the Wright brothers’ 1903 airplane."});

ADDART({id:"vostok",t:"The Vostok and Voskhod Programs",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🇷🇺",w:"Vostok programme",r:["space","sputnik","womeninspace","spacewalks","projectmercury"],
p:[["First humans in space","The Soviet Vostok program launched the first person into space."],["Gagarin and Tereshkova","Yuri Gagarin flew in 1961 and Valentina Tereshkova in 1963."],["The first spacewalk","Voskhod 2 carried Alexei Leonov on the first spacewalk."]],
l:"The Vostok program was the Soviet Union’s first human spaceflight program, which put the first person and the first woman into space. Led by chief designer Sergei Korolev, whose identity was kept secret, it used spherical capsules launched on rockets developed from intercontinental missiles. On 12 April 1961, Vostok 1 carried cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin once around Earth in 108 minutes, making him the first human in space and a worldwide hero. In August 1961, Gherman Titov spent more than a day in orbit on Vostok 2. In 1962, Vostok 3 and Vostok 4 flew at the same time, and on 16 June 1963, Valentina Tereshkova became the first woman in space aboard Vostok 6, orbiting Earth 48 times. The follow-up Voskhod program squeezed three cosmonauts without spacesuits into a modified capsule in 1964. On 18 March 1965, during Voskhod 2, Alexei Leonov made the first spacewalk, struggling to get back inside when his spacesuit ballooned. These achievements pushed the United States to speed up its own program in the space race. The Soviets later moved on to the Soyuz spacecraft, still used today.",
s:[["The Vostok capsule","Vostok’s ball-shaped descent module could not steer through the atmosphere, so cosmonauts ejected at about 7 kilometers altitude and landed separately by parachute. This was kept secret for years because international rules for records required pilots to land with their craft."],["Yuri Gagarin","Gagarin, the son of a collective farm worker, became a global celebrity. His cheerful ‘Poyekhali!’ (‘Let’s go!’) at liftoff became famous. He died in a jet crash during a training flight in 1968. 12 April is celebrated as Cosmonautics Day in Russia."],["Valentina Tereshkova","Tereshkova was a textile worker and amateur parachutist chosen from a group of women trainees. She remained the only woman to fly in space until Svetlana Savitskaya in 1982."],["Leonov’s spacewalk","Leonov spent about 12 minutes outside Voskhod 2. His suit swelled so much in the vacuum that he had to let out some air to squeeze back into the airlock, a dangerous moment kept secret for years."]],
k:[["First human in space","Yuri Gagarin, 12 April 1961"],["First woman in space","Valentina Tereshkova, 16 June 1963"],["First spacewalk","Alexei Leonov, 18 March 1965"],["Chief designer","Sergei Korolev"],["Vostok 1 flight time","108 minutes"]],
d:[["1961","Gagarin’s Vostok 1 flight"],["1961","Titov spends a day in orbit"],["1962","Vostok 3 and 4 fly together"],["1963","Tereshkova flies Vostok 6"],["1964","Voskhod 1 carries three cosmonauts"],["1965","Leonov’s first spacewalk"]],
s2:[["Sergei Korolev","Korolev survived imprisonment in a Soviet labor camp in the 1930s and went on to lead the design of Sputnik and Vostok. His name was revealed publicly only after his death in 1966."],["Secret landing","Gagarin landed near the Volga River and met a surprised farmer and her granddaughter, telling them he was a Soviet who had come from space."]],
sp:["‘Let’s go!’","Gagarin’s launch call ‘Poyekhali!’ has become a famous phrase in Russia, and the anniversary of his flight is celebrated worldwide as the International Day of Human Space Flight."],
g:[["Cosmonaut","A Soviet or Russian astronaut"],["Capsule","A small crewed spacecraft"],["Spacewalk","Activity outside a spacecraft in space"],["Space race","Competition between the US and USSR in space"]],
f:["Yuri Gagarin was the first human in space.","Valentina Tereshkova was the first woman in space.","Alexei Leonov made the first spacewalk."],
fun:"Yuri Gagarin ejected from his capsule and landed by parachute, a detail kept secret for years."});

ADDART({id:"salyut",t:"Salyut Space Stations",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Salyut programme",r:["iss","mir","skylab","soyuz","tiangong"],
p:[["The first space stations","Salyut 1 became the world’s first space station in 1971."],["Tragedy and triumph","The first crew died returning home, but later stations succeeded."],["Paving the way","Salyut led to Mir and the International Space Station."]],
l:"The Salyut program was a series of Soviet space stations launched between 1971 and 1982, including the world’s first space station. Salyut 1 was launched on 19 April 1971. In June 1971, the crew of Soyuz 11, Georgy Dobrovolsky, Vladislav Volkov and Viktor Patsayev, spent 23 days aboard, a record at the time, but they died when a valve opened and their capsule lost its air during the return to Earth. They remain the only people to have died in space. Some Salyuts were secretly military stations, called Almaz, designed for reconnaissance; one reportedly carried a modified aircraft cannon. Later civilian stations, Salyut 6 (1977–1982) and Salyut 7 (1982–1991), had two docking ports, allowing cargo ships called Progress to deliver supplies and visiting crews to arrive while residents stayed aboard. This let cosmonauts stay for months and hosted guest cosmonauts from countries allied with the Soviet Union, as well as France and India. In 1985, cosmonauts Vladimir Dzhanibekov and Viktor Savinykh rescued the frozen, powerless Salyut 7 in a daring repair mission. Salyut experience led directly to the modular Mir station and to the Russian parts of the International Space Station.",
s:[["Soyuz 11","The Soyuz 11 cosmonauts were not wearing pressure suits during reentry because there was no room in the capsule. When a valve opened at separation, the cabin lost its air in seconds. After the disaster, Soviet crews always wore pressure suits during launch and landing."],["Long-duration flights","Salyut stations let scientists study how the human body adapts to months of weightlessness. Cosmonauts exercised daily and grew plants, and missions grew to more than 200 days."],["International guests","Through the Interkosmos program, cosmonauts from Czechoslovakia, Poland, East Germany, Cuba, Vietnam, Mongolia and other countries visited Salyut stations. In 1984, Rakesh Sharma became the first Indian in space aboard Salyut 7."],["Saving Salyut 7","In 1985, Salyut 7 lost power and froze. Dzhanibekov and Savinykh docked manually with the tumbling station, wore fur hats and warm clothes in its freezing interior and brought it back to life. The rescue was dramatized in a 2017 Russian film."]],
k:[["First space station","Salyut 1, 19 April 1971"],["Soyuz 11 stay","23 days"],["Military stations","Almaz"],["Two docking ports","Salyut 6 and 7"],["Successor","Mir (1986)"]],
d:[["1971","Salyut 1 launched"],["1971","Soyuz 11 crew dies returning home"],["1977","Salyut 6 launched"],["1982","Salyut 7 launched"],["1985","Salyut 7 rescue mission"],["1991","Salyut 7 reenters the atmosphere"]],
s2:[["Salyut 7’s fall","Salyut 7 fell back to Earth in February 1991, with some pieces landing in Argentina. No one was hurt."],["Skylab","The United States launched its own station, Skylab, in 1973. While Salyut stations evolved step by step, Skylab was a single large station visited by three crews."]],
sp:["Remembered on the Moon","The names of the Soyuz 11 cosmonauts are on the Fallen Astronaut memorial plaque left on the Moon by Apollo 15 astronauts in 1971."],
g:[["Space station","A spacecraft where crews live for long periods"],["Docking port","A connection point for spacecraft"],["Progress","An uncrewed Soviet and Russian cargo spacecraft"],["Reentry","Returning into Earth’s atmosphere"]],
f:["Salyut 1 was the first space station.","The Soyuz 11 crew are the only people to die in space.","Salyut led to Mir and the ISS."],
fun:"Cosmonauts rescuing the frozen Salyut 7 in 1985 worked inside it wearing fur hats."});

ADDART({id:"buran",t:"Buran: The Soviet Space Shuttle",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛩️",w:"Buran (spacecraft)",r:["spaceshuttle","soyuz","space","rocket","ussrfall"],
p:[["The Soviet shuttle","Buran was the Soviet Union’s answer to the US Space Shuttle."],["One flight, no crew","It flew once, uncrewed, in 1988 and landed automatically."],["A sad end","The program was canceled, and the orbiter was destroyed in 2002."]],
l:"Buran, meaning ‘snowstorm’ or ‘blizzard’, was a reusable spaceplane developed by the Soviet Union in response to the United States’ Space Shuttle. It looked very similar to the American shuttle, partly because the shapes suited the same physics and partly because Soviet engineers studied American designs. However, there were important differences: Buran had no main engines of its own. Instead, it rode on the side of Energia, a powerful separate rocket that could also launch other heavy payloads. On 15 November 1988, Buran launched from the Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan without a crew, orbited Earth twice and landed automatically on a runway, an impressive achievement for computer control at the time. It never flew again. After the collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991, funding dried up, and the program was officially canceled in 1993. On 12 May 2002, the roof of the hangar at Baikonur where the flown Buran was stored collapsed, destroying the orbiter and killing eight workers. Several unfinished Buran test vehicles survive, some abandoned in hangars and one displayed at a technical museum in Germany.",
s:[["Energia","The Energia rocket was one of the most powerful ever built, able to lift about 100 metric tons to low Earth orbit. It flew only twice: in 1987 with a military satellite that failed to reach orbit, and in 1988 with Buran."],["Automatic landing","Buran’s computers flew it through reentry and landed it within a few meters of the runway centerline, even adjusting for strong crosswinds. American shuttles were landed by astronauts."],["Why build it?","Soviet leaders worried that the US shuttle could have military uses, such as capturing satellites. Buran was intended to match any such capability and to carry crews and cargo to future stations."],["Cost","The Buran–Energia program was enormously expensive at a time when the Soviet economy was struggling, and it is sometimes cited as an example of the costs of the Cold War arms race."]],
k:[["Meaning","Snowstorm"],["Only flight","15 November 1988 (uncrewed)"],["Orbits","Two"],["Launch rocket","Energia"],["Destroyed","12 May 2002, hangar collapse"]],
d:[["1976","Buran program begins"],["1987","First Energia launch"],["1988","Buran’s only flight"],["1991","Soviet Union collapses"],["1993","Program canceled"],["2002","Flown Buran destroyed in hangar collapse"]],
s2:[["Abandoned shuttles","Two unfinished Buran orbiters sat for years in a hangar at Baikonur, visited by urban explorers who photographed them under layers of dust and bird droppings."],["Buran at the Paris Air Show","In 1989, Buran was carried on the back of the giant Antonov An-225 Mriya, the world’s largest aircraft, to the Paris Air Show. The An-225 was destroyed in Ukraine in 2022."]],
sp:["The world’s largest plane","The six-engine Antonov An-225 Mriya was built specifically to carry Buran and parts of Energia on its back."],
g:[["Spaceplane","A spacecraft that lands like an airplane"],["Orbiter","The part of a shuttle that goes into orbit"],["Cosmodrome","A Soviet or Russian spaceport"],["Uncrewed","Without people aboard"]],
f:["Buran flew once, in 1988.","It landed automatically.","It was destroyed in a hangar collapse in 2002."],
fun:"Buran flew to space and landed on a runway with no one aboard, steered entirely by computers."});

ADDART({id:"longmarch",t:"Long March Rockets",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🇨🇳",w:"Long March (rocket family)",r:["chinacountry","tiangong","changeprogram","tianwen","spaceagencies"],
p:[["China’s rockets","Long March rockets launch most of China’s spacecraft."],["First satellite in 1970","Long March 1 launched China’s first satellite."],["Toward the Moon","Long March 10 is being developed to carry astronauts to the Moon."]],
l:"Long March (Chang Zheng in Chinese) is the family of rockets used by China to launch satellites, space station modules, crews and probes. Named after the Long March, the Chinese Communist Party’s military retreat of 1934–1935, the rockets were developed from ballistic missiles. On 24 April 1970, a Long March 1 rocket launched Dong Fang Hong 1, China’s first satellite, which broadcast the song ‘The East Is Red’ from orbit, making China the fifth country to launch its own satellite. Since then, many versions have been developed. The Long March 2F launches Shenzhou spacecraft with astronauts, starting with Yang Liwei, China’s first astronaut, in 2003. The heavy Long March 5, first flown in 2016, launched China’s Tianwen-1 Mars mission, the Chang’e 5 and Chang’e 6 lunar sample return missions and modules of the Tiangong space station. China launches Long March rockets from four sites, Jiuquan, Taiyuan, Xichang and the coastal Wenchang. For future crewed Moon landings, which China aims to achieve before 2030, it is developing the Long March 10; a low-altitude flight test in February 2026 also tested the escape system of the new Mengzhou crew spacecraft. Chinese companies are also developing reusable rockets.",
s:[["Launch sites","Jiuquan, in the Gobi Desert, launches crews. Xichang, in the mountains of Sichuan, launches satellites to high orbits. Taiyuan launches into polar orbits, and Wenchang, on Hainan Island, launches the heaviest rockets because its coastal location allows large rockets to be shipped by sea."],["Falling stages","Because inland sites launch over land, spent rocket stages sometimes fall near villages, occasionally causing damage. China has tested grid fins and parachutes to steer stages to safer areas."],["Accidents","In 1996, a Long March 3B veered off course and crashed into a village near Xichang, killing at least six people according to official figures. After this, reliability improved greatly."],["Busy launch schedule","China now launches dozens of Long March rockets a year, second only to the United States, building satellite networks and supporting its space station and Moon program."]],
k:[["Chinese name","Chang Zheng"],["First launch","24 April 1970 (Dong Fang Hong 1)"],["Crew rocket","Long March 2F"],["Heavy rocket","Long March 5 (2016)"],["Moon rocket","Long March 10 (in development)"]],
d:[["1970","Long March 1 launches China’s first satellite"],["1996","Long March 3B launch failure"],["2003","Long March 2F launches Yang Liwei"],["2016","First Long March 5 launch"],["2020","Long March 5 launches Tianwen-1 and Chang’e 5"],["2026","Long March 10 low-altitude flight test"]],
s2:[["The East Is Red","Dong Fang Hong 1 was designed to be seen and heard: a reflective ring made it visible to the naked eye, and it played the song ‘The East Is Red’ on radio. It weighed 173 kilograms, more than the first satellites of the Soviet Union, the United States, France and Japan combined."],["Commercial rockets","Since the 2010s, private Chinese companies such as LandSpace have developed their own rockets, including methane-fueled designs, competing alongside the state-built Long March family."]],
sp:["Still in orbit","Dong Fang Hong 1 stopped transmitting after about 26 days, but the satellite itself is still in orbit more than 55 years later."],
g:[["Ballistic missile","A rocket-powered weapon"],["Launch site","A place where rockets are launched"],["Spent stage","A used rocket section that falls away"],["Shenzhou","China’s crewed spacecraft"]],
f:["Long March 1 launched China’s first satellite in 1970.","Long March 2F launches Chinese astronauts.","Long March 10 is being built for Moon missions."],
fun:"China’s first satellite played the song ‘The East Is Red’ from orbit in 1970, and it is still circling Earth."});

ADDART({id:"jaxa",t:"Japan’s Space Program",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🇯🇵",w:"JAXA",r:["japancountry","hayabusa","spaceagencies","iss","phobosdeimos"],
p:[["From pencil rockets to asteroids","Japan began with tiny rockets in 1955."],["Pinpoint Moon landing","SLIM made Japan the fifth country to land on the Moon in 2024."],["JAXA","The Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency was formed in 2003."]],
l:"Japan’s space program, led today by the Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency (JAXA), is known for creative science missions, especially to asteroids. It began in 1955, when engineer Hideo Itokawa and his team at the University of Tokyo launched tiny ‘pencil rockets’ only 23 centimeters long. On 11 February 1970, Japan launched its first satellite, Ohsumi, becoming the fourth country to put a satellite into orbit with its own rocket. JAXA was formed on 1 October 2003 by merging three organizations. Japan built the Kibo laboratory, the largest single module on the International Space Station, and HTV cargo spacecraft that supplied the station. Its Hayabusa missions brought back samples from the asteroids Itokawa and Ryugu, and the Akatsuki probe has studied Venus. On 20 January 2024, the Smart Lander for Investigating Moon (SLIM) made Japan the fifth country to soft-land on the Moon, touching down within about 100 meters of its target, though it landed upside down. Japan’s new H3 rocket succeeded on its second flight in February 2024, and in 2026 it launched the Martian Moons eXploration (MMX) mission to bring back a sample from Phobos. Japanese astronauts have flown on many missions to the ISS.",
s:[["Pencil rockets","Itokawa’s pencil rockets were fired horizontally in 1955 to test rocket design. The asteroid Itokawa, visited by Hayabusa, is named after him, and he is called the father of Japanese rocketry."],["Asteroid explorers","Hayabusa returned the first asteroid samples to Earth in 2010, and Hayabusa2 returned samples from Ryugu in 2020. Ryugu samples contained amino acids and water-bearing minerals."],["SLIM","SLIM, nicknamed the ‘Moon Sniper’, used image-matching navigation to land precisely. Although one engine failed and it tipped over, its solar panels still produced power on some days, and it survived several freezing lunar nights."],["Astronauts","Mamoru Mohri flew on the Space Shuttle in 1992, and Chiaki Mukai became Japan’s first woman in space in 1994. Koichi Wakata became the first Japanese commander of the ISS in 2014."]],
k:[["Pencil rocket tests","1955"],["First satellite","Ohsumi, 11 February 1970"],["JAXA founded","1 October 2003"],["SLIM Moon landing","20 January 2024"],["Main rocket","H3"]],
d:[["1955","Pencil rockets tested"],["1970","Ohsumi launched"],["2003","JAXA formed"],["2008","Kibo module attached to the ISS"],["2010","Hayabusa returns asteroid samples"],["2024","SLIM lands on the Moon; H3 succeeds"],["2026","MMX launched toward Mars’s moons"]],
s2:[["Kibo","Kibo, meaning ‘hope’, includes a pressurized laboratory, a storage section, a robotic arm and an outdoor platform for experiments exposed to space. Astronauts have used it to launch small satellites."],["Space anime","Japan’s space program has often been popular with the public, and JAXA missions have been celebrated with anime-style mascots and fan art, especially Hayabusa, the subject of several films."]],
sp:["Upside-down success","Photos from a small rover released by SLIM showed the lander resting upside down on the Moon, its nozzle pointing up, but it still sent back science data."],
g:[["JAXA","Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency"],["Soft landing","Landing gently without crashing"],["Kibo","Japan’s laboratory on the ISS"],["Sample return","Bringing material from space to Earth"]],
f:["Japan launched its first satellite in 1970.","SLIM landed on the Moon in 2024.","Hayabusa returned asteroid samples."],
fun:"Japan’s ‘Moon Sniper’ lander hit its target precisely in 2024 but ended up resting upside down."});

ADDART({id:"weathersatellites",t:"Weather Satellites",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌀",w:"Weather satellite",r:["weatherforecast","sat","storms","climate","orbits","satellites"],
p:[["Watching the weather from space","Weather satellites photograph clouds and storms around the world."],["TIROS-1","The first weather satellite launched in 1960."],["Saving lives","Satellites help forecasters warn people of typhoons and hurricanes."]],
l:"Weather satellites observe Earth’s atmosphere, clouds, oceans and land to help forecast weather and monitor climate. The first successful weather satellite, NASA’s TIROS-1, launched on 1 April 1960 and sent back thousands of television pictures of clouds, revealing weather systems as never before. There are two main types. Geostationary satellites orbit about 36,000 kilometers above the equator, moving with Earth’s rotation so they constantly watch the same region, taking images every few minutes; examples include the US GOES series, Japan’s Himawari, Europe’s Meteosat and China’s Fengyun. Polar-orbiting satellites fly much lower, about 800 kilometers up, passing over the poles and scanning the whole Earth twice a day in greater detail. Satellites measure clouds, temperature, humidity, rainfall, winds over the ocean, sea surface temperatures, snow and ice, and even lightning. Their data feed computer forecast models and allow forecasters to track tropical cyclones from their earliest stages, giving days of warning before landfall, which has saved countless lives. Weather satellites also provide the long-term records scientists use to study climate change.",
s:[["Geostationary satellites","Positioned at fixed points over the equator, geostationary satellites provide the familiar animated cloud images on weather reports. A ring of about five or six satellites run by different countries covers most of the planet, except near the poles."],["Polar orbiters","Polar-orbiting satellites carry instruments that measure temperature and moisture at different heights in the atmosphere. These measurements are among the most important inputs to global forecast models."],["Tracking storms","Before satellites, tropical cyclones could form unseen over the ocean. Now forecasters watch them develop and estimate their strength from cloud patterns, though aircraft still fly into storms in some regions for direct measurements."],["International cooperation","Countries share weather satellite data through the World Meteorological Organization, so forecasters everywhere benefit, including in countries without their own satellites."]],
k:[["First weather satellite","TIROS-1, 1 April 1960"],["Geostationary altitude","About 36,000 kilometers"],["Polar orbit altitude","About 800 kilometers"],["Examples","GOES, Himawari, Meteosat, Fengyun"],["Data sharing","World Meteorological Organization"]],
d:[["1960","TIROS-1 launched"],["1966","First geostationary weather images (ATS-1)"],["1975","First GOES satellite launched"],["1977","Japan’s first Himawari and Europe’s first Meteosat launched"],["2016","GOES-16 begins a new generation of US satellites"]],
s2:[["Himawari","Japan’s Himawari satellites, whose name means ‘sunflower’, watch East Asia, Australia and the western Pacific, including the typhoons that affect the Philippines, Japan and China."],["Lightning mappers","Newer geostationary satellites carry instruments that detect lightning flashes day and night, helping forecasters identify strengthening storms."]],
sp:["First picture","TIROS-1’s first image, a fuzzy view of clouds over the northeastern United States, was shown to President Dwight Eisenhower the day it was taken."],
g:[["Geostationary orbit","An orbit that keeps a satellite over one spot"],["Polar orbit","An orbit passing over the poles"],["Tropical cyclone","A typhoon or hurricane"],["Forecast model","A computer simulation of the atmosphere"]],
f:["TIROS-1 launched in 1960.","Geostationary satellites watch the same area constantly.","Satellites give days of warning before typhoons."],
fun:"Japan’s weather satellites are named Himawari, meaning ‘sunflower’, because they always face Earth as a sunflower faces the Sun."});

ADDART({id:"earthobservation",t:"Landsat and Earth Observation",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌍",w:"Landsat program",r:["sat","deforestation","climate","maps","weathersatellites"],
p:[["Watching our planet","Earth observation satellites photograph and measure Earth’s surface."],["Landsat since 1972","Landsat has recorded Earth’s land for more than 50 years."],["Free data","Free satellite images help track forests, farms and disasters."]],
l:"Earth observation satellites photograph and measure Earth’s land, oceans, ice and atmosphere to monitor changes in the environment, farming, cities and natural disasters. The first satellite images used for this purpose came from spy satellites such as the US Corona program, which began in 1960 and dropped film capsules from orbit. NASA launched Landsat 1, the first satellite designed to study Earth’s land surface, on 23 July 1972. The Landsat series has continued ever since, with Landsat 8 launched in 2013 and Landsat 9 in 2021, creating the longest continuous record of Earth’s land from space. In 2008, the United States made Landsat images free to everyone, and their use exploded. Europe’s Copernicus program, with its Sentinel satellites launched since 2014, also provides free data. Satellites use cameras that see colors invisible to the eye, such as infrared, which reveals healthy plants, and radar, which sees through clouds and at night. Earth observation is used to track deforestation, melting glaciers, crop health, floods, wildfires, urban growth and illegal fishing. Companies such as Planet operate fleets of small satellites that photograph nearly all of Earth’s land every day.",
s:[["Seeing in many colors","Landsat and Sentinel cameras record several bands of light, including near-infrared, which healthy plants reflect strongly. Combining bands creates false-color images that show vegetation, water and burned land clearly."],["Radar satellites","Synthetic aperture radar satellites send out microwave pulses and measure their echoes. They can map floods through clouds, measure ground sinking by millimeters and track ships at night."],["Uses","Governments use satellite data to plan farms, monitor droughts and respond to disasters. Scientists track the shrinking of glaciers and the Amazon rainforest, and journalists and human rights groups use images to document conflicts."],["Measuring water and gravity","The GRACE satellites, launched in 2002, measured tiny changes in Earth’s gravity caused by moving water, revealing groundwater loss and melting ice sheets."]],
k:[["Landsat 1 launched","23 July 1972"],["Latest Landsat","Landsat 9 (2021)"],["Landsat data free","Since 2008"],["European program","Copernicus (Sentinel satellites)"],["Key technologies","Multispectral cameras and radar"]],
d:[["1960","First successful Corona spy satellite mission"],["1972","Landsat 1 launched"],["2002","GRACE launched"],["2008","Landsat data made free"],["2014","First Copernicus Sentinel satellite launched"],["2021","Landsat 9 launched"]],
s2:[["The Blue Marble","On 7 December 1972, the Apollo 17 crew photographed the whole sunlit Earth, an image called The Blue Marble that became one of the most reproduced photographs in history and a symbol of environmental awareness."],["Finding new islands","Landsat images revealed Landsat Island off Canada’s coast in 1976, a small island found by satellite and named after it."]],
sp:["Earth as art","NASA and the US Geological Survey publish collections of Landsat images chosen for their beauty, showing river deltas, deserts and glaciers that look like abstract paintings."],
g:[["Earth observation","Gathering information about Earth from space"],["Multispectral","Recording several bands of light"],["False color","Using colors to represent invisible light"],["Synthetic aperture radar","Radar that creates detailed images from orbit"]],
f:["Landsat 1 launched in 1972.","Landsat images have been free since 2008.","Radar satellites can see through clouds."],
fun:"A small island off Canada was discovered in Landsat images and named Landsat Island."});

ADDART({id:"sagittariusa",t:"Sagittarius A*",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"⚫",w:"Sagittarius A*",r:["bh","milky","eht","relat","starclusters","supermassivebh"],
p:[["Our galaxy’s black hole","Sagittarius A* is the supermassive black hole at the center of the Milky Way."],["4 million Suns","It has about 4 million times the mass of the Sun."],["Nobel-winning discovery","Tracking stars around it won the 2020 Nobel Prize in Physics."]],
l:"Sagittarius A* (pronounced ‘Sagittarius A-star’) is the supermassive black hole at the center of our Milky Way galaxy, about 26,000 light-years from Earth in the direction of the constellation Sagittarius. It has a mass about 4 million times that of the Sun, packed into a region smaller than Mercury’s orbit. Astronomers discovered a compact radio source at the galaxy’s center in 1974 and gave it its name. Thick dust blocks visible light from the center, but infrared telescopes can see through it. Starting in the 1990s, two teams, led by Reinhard Genzel in Germany and Andrea Ghez in the United States, tracked stars racing around the invisible object; one star, S2, completes an orbit every 16 years, reaching speeds of several thousand kilometers per second. Their measurements showed that only a black hole could be so massive and compact, and they shared the 2020 Nobel Prize in Physics with Roger Penrose. On 12 May 2022, the Event Horizon Telescope released the first image of Sagittarius A*, showing a glowing ring around its dark shadow. Compared with black holes in some other galaxies, Sagittarius A* is quiet, but it flickers with flares several times a day.",
s:[["Watching stars orbit","By measuring star positions with adaptive optics, which cancel the blurring of Earth’s atmosphere, astronomers traced the orbits of dozens of stars. In 2018, S2’s close pass showed light losing energy in the black hole’s gravity, as Einstein’s relativity predicts."],["A quiet giant","Sagittarius A* swallows relatively little gas, so it is much dimmer than the active black holes that power quasars. Evidence suggests it was more active in the past, perhaps blowing out giant bubbles of gas detected above and below the galaxy."],["Flares","Sagittarius A* brightens in X-rays and infrared light several times a day, probably as magnetic energy is released in the hot gas around it. The James Webb Space Telescope has observed constant flickering."],["No danger","Sagittarius A* is far too distant to pose any threat to Earth. Our solar system orbits the galactic center safely, taking about 230 million years for each orbit."]],
k:[["Distance","About 26,000 light-years"],["Mass","About 4 million Suns"],["Discovered as radio source","1974"],["Nobel Prize","2020 (Genzel and Ghez, with Penrose)"],["First image","12 May 2022 (Event Horizon Telescope)"]],
d:[["1974","Compact radio source discovered at the galactic center"],["1990s","Teams begin tracking stars around it"],["2002","Star S2 seen making a close pass"],["2018","S2’s close pass confirms relativity"],["2020","Nobel Prize for the discovery"],["2022","First image released"]],
s2:[["Andrea Ghez","Andrea Ghez became the fourth woman ever to win the Nobel Prize in Physics, after Marie Curie, Maria Goeppert Mayer and Donna Strickland."],["Hypervelocity stars","Some stars are flung out of the galactic center at extremely high speeds, probably after a pair of stars passes too close to Sagittarius A* and one is captured while the other is ejected."]],
sp:["The asterisk","The asterisk in the name comes from atomic physics notation for an excited state; astronomer Robert Brown chose it in 1982 because the source was so exciting."],
g:[["Supermassive black hole","A black hole millions or billions of times the Sun’s mass"],["Galactic center","The middle of a galaxy"],["Adaptive optics","Technology that removes atmospheric blurring"],["Flare","A sudden burst of brightness"]],
f:["Sagittarius A* is about 26,000 light-years away.","It weighs about 4 million Suns.","Its image was released in 2022."],
fun:"The star S2 races around Sagittarius A* at speeds of thousands of kilometers per second, completing an orbit every 16 years."});

ADDART({id:"planetnine",t:"Planet Nine",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"❓",w:"Planet Nine",r:["kuiper","dwarfplanets","pluto","uranep","groundtelescopes"],
p:[["A hidden planet?","Some astronomers think an undiscovered planet orbits far beyond Neptune."],["Clues in distant orbits","Odd orbits of distant icy objects hint at its gravity."],["Still not found","As of 2026, no one has seen Planet Nine."]],
l:"Planet Nine is a hypothetical large planet that some astronomers think may orbit the Sun far beyond Neptune. In January 2016, Konstantin Batygin and Mike Brown of the California Institute of Technology proposed it to explain a puzzling pattern: several very distant objects in the Kuiper Belt, the region of icy bodies beyond Neptune, have stretched orbits that point in similar directions and are tilted in similar ways, as if shepherded by the gravity of a hidden planet. According to their models, Planet Nine might be about five to ten times the mass of Earth and orbit hundreds of times farther from the Sun than Earth does, taking 10,000 years or more to complete one orbit. At that distance, it would be very faint, reflecting little sunlight, so it would be hard to spot. Skeptics argue that the clustering of orbits may be an illusion caused by where and when telescopes have looked, and that other explanations, such as the combined gravity of many small objects, are possible. The Vera C. Rubin Observatory in Chile, which began its sky survey in 2025, is expected to find many more distant objects and could either discover Planet Nine or rule out much of where it could be. As of 2026, it has not been found.",
s:[["The clues","Objects such as Sedna, discovered in 2003, and others found later never come close to Neptune, so Neptune cannot explain their stretched orbits. The proposed Planet Nine would keep their orbits aligned over billions of years."],["Where would it come from?","Planet Nine might have formed closer to the Sun and been pushed outward by Jupiter and Saturn early in the solar system’s history, or it might have been captured from another star in the Sun’s birth cluster."],["Searching","Astronomers have searched old survey images and used large telescopes such as Subaru in Hawaii. In infrared light, a planet’s own warmth might make it visible. So far, searches have ruled out some regions of the sky."],["Previous planet hunts","Neptune was found in 1846 after astronomers predicted its position from irregularities in Uranus’s orbit. A later search for a ‘Planet X’ beyond Neptune led to the discovery of Pluto in 1930, though Pluto proved too small to be what they sought."]],
k:[["Proposed","January 2016"],["Proposed by","Konstantin Batygin and Mike Brown"],["Estimated mass","About 5–10 Earths"],["Estimated orbit","10,000 years or more"],["Status (2026)","Not found"]],
d:[["1846","Neptune discovered by prediction"],["1930","Pluto discovered during a search for Planet X"],["2003","Sedna discovered"],["2014","Unusual alignment of distant objects noted"],["2016","Planet Nine proposed"],["2025","Rubin Observatory begins its survey"]],
s2:[["The planet killer","Mike Brown led the team that discovered Eris in 2005, a discovery that led to Pluto’s reclassification as a dwarf planet. His Twitter handle was ‘plutokiller’, and he joked that finding Planet Nine would bring the number of planets back to nine."],["Primordial black hole?","Some physicists have suggested that the unseen object could be a tiny black hole about the size of a grapefruit, captured by the Sun, though most astronomers consider this unlikely."]],
sp:["A faint target","If Planet Nine exists at its predicted distance, it could be thousands of times fainter than Pluto, making it like looking for a dark coal lump in the night sky."],
g:[["Hypothetical","Proposed but not proven"],["Kuiper Belt","A region of icy bodies beyond Neptune"],["Orbit clustering","Orbits pointing in similar directions"],["Dwarf planet","A small planet-like body that has not cleared its orbit"]],
f:["Planet Nine was proposed in 2016.","It might be 5–10 times Earth’s mass.","It had not been found as of 2026."],
fun:"Some scientists have suggested Planet Nine might actually be a grapefruit-sized black hole, though most think this is unlikely."});

ADDART({id:"saturnrings",t:"Saturn’s Rings",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"💍",w:"Rings of Saturn",r:["saturn","cassini","galileo","moons","titan"],
p:[["The solar system’s jewel","Saturn’s rings are the largest and brightest planetary rings."],["Made of ice","They are made of countless chunks of water ice."],["Wide but thin","They stretch hundreds of thousands of kilometers but are often only about 10 meters thick."]],
l:"Saturn’s rings are a vast, flat system of countless particles of water ice, with some rock and dust, orbiting the planet above its equator. Ring particles range from tiny grains to chunks as big as houses. The main rings stretch about 280,000 kilometers across, yet in many places they are only about 10 meters thick, making them proportionally thinner than a sheet of paper. In 1610, Galileo Galilei saw something strange around Saturn with his small telescope and described the planet as having ‘ears’; in 1655, Dutch astronomer Christiaan Huygens explained that Saturn is surrounded by a thin, flat ring. In 1675, Giovanni Cassini discovered a gap, now called the Cassini Division, separating the two brightest rings. Saturn’s rings are named with letters in order of discovery, from the faint D ring closest to Saturn to the distant E ring. Small moons orbiting within or near the rings, called shepherd moons, shape their edges and create waves. The Voyager probes and especially the Cassini spacecraft, which orbited Saturn from 2004 to 2017, revealed intricate structures. Scientists still debate whether the rings are ancient or formed relatively recently from a shattered moon or comet.",
s:[["Ring plane crossings","Because Saturn is tilted, we see its rings from different angles as it orbits the Sun every 29.5 years. About every 15 years, the rings line up edge-on to Earth and nearly vanish from view, as happened in March 2025."],["How old are the rings?","Cassini measured the rings’ mass and found them surprisingly clean, suggesting they could be only a few hundred million years old, younger than the dinosaurs. Other scientists argue that rings could stay clean for billions of years, and the question remains open."],["Ring rain","Cassini found that material from the rings is falling into Saturn, pulled by gravity and the magnetic field, a process called ‘ring rain’. Over a few hundred million years, the rings may largely disappear."],["Other ringed planets","Jupiter, Uranus and Neptune also have rings, but they are much fainter and darker. Rings have even been found around some dwarf planets and small icy bodies, such as Haumea and Chariklo."]],
k:[["Main rings width","About 280,000 kilometers"],["Typical thickness","About 10 meters"],["Main material","Water ice"],["First described as a ring","Christiaan Huygens, 1655"],["Cassini Division discovered","1675"]],
d:[["1610","Galileo sees Saturn’s ‘ears’"],["1655","Huygens explains the ring"],["1675","Cassini discovers the Cassini Division"],["1980","Voyager 1 reveals complex ring structure"],["2004","Cassini spacecraft arrives at Saturn"],["2017","Cassini’s Grand Finale dives between the rings"],["2025","Rings appear edge-on from Earth"]],
s2:[["Spokes","Voyager discovered dark, ghostly ‘spokes’ that appear across the rings and fade, probably caused by electrically charged dust lifted above the ring plane. They come and go with Saturn’s seasons."],["The Grand Finale","In 2017, Cassini made 22 daring dives through the gap between Saturn and its innermost rings before plunging into Saturn’s atmosphere on 15 September 2017."]],
sp:["A frozen bath","If all the ring material were gathered together, it would make a moon only a few hundred kilometers across, smaller than many of Saturn’s moons."],
g:[["Ring particle","A piece of ice or rock orbiting in a ring"],["Shepherd moon","A small moon that shapes a ring"],["Ring plane crossing","When rings are seen edge-on"],["Cassini Division","The largest gap in Saturn’s rings"]],
f:["Saturn’s rings are mostly water ice.","They are often only about 10 meters thick.","They nearly vanished from view in March 2025."],
fun:"Saturn’s rings are so thin compared with their width that a scale model made of paper would be too thick."});

ADDART({id:"solarwind",t:"The Solar Wind and the Heliosphere",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌬️",w:"Solar wind",r:["sun","spaceweather","voyager","parkerprobe","comet"],
p:[["Wind from the Sun","The Sun constantly blows charged particles into space."],["A giant bubble","The solar wind inflates the heliosphere around the solar system."],["Leaving the bubble","Voyager 1 crossed into interstellar space in 2012."]],
l:"The solar wind is a stream of charged particles, mainly electrons and protons, that flows constantly outward from the Sun’s hot outer atmosphere, the corona, in all directions. It typically travels at about 400 kilometers per second, with faster streams of about 750 kilometers per second coming from dark regions called coronal holes. American physicist Eugene Parker predicted the solar wind in 1958, an idea many scientists at first rejected. Soviet Luna probes detected it in 1959, and NASA’s Mariner 2 confirmed it on its way to Venus in 1962. The solar wind carries the Sun’s magnetic field outward and inflates a vast bubble around the solar system called the heliosphere, which shields the planets from some cosmic rays from the galaxy. The boundary where the solar wind meets the gas between the stars is called the heliopause. NASA’s Voyager 1 crossed it on 25 August 2012, becoming the first human-made object to enter interstellar space, and Voyager 2 followed in 2018. The solar wind causes comet tails to point away from the Sun and, when gusts strike Earth’s magnetic field, produces auroras and space weather. NASA’s IMAP mission, launched in September 2025, is mapping the heliosphere’s boundary.",
s:[["Why the Sun has a wind","The corona is heated to more than a million degrees Celsius, far hotter than the Sun’s surface. At such temperatures, particles move so fast that the Sun’s gravity cannot hold them, and they stream away into space."],["Comet tails","Comets have two tails: a curved dust tail and a straight, bluish ion tail made of gas pushed directly away from the Sun by the solar wind. Observations of comet tails gave early clues that a solar wind existed."],["The heliosphere","The heliosphere extends well beyond Pluto. As the Sun moves through the galaxy, the heliosphere may be shaped somewhat like a comet or a croissant, though scientists still debate its exact form."],["Eugene Parker","Parker’s 1958 paper was nearly rejected by reviewers, but the journal’s editor, Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar, published it. NASA’s Parker Solar Probe, launched in 2018, was the first spacecraft named after a living person, and Parker watched its launch."]],
k:[["Typical speed","About 400 km per second"],["Fast wind","About 750 km per second"],["Predicted","Eugene Parker, 1958"],["Voyager 1 entered interstellar space","25 August 2012"],["Heliosphere mapper","IMAP (launched 2025)"]],
d:[["1958","Parker predicts the solar wind"],["1959","Luna probes detect the solar wind"],["1962","Mariner 2 confirms the solar wind"],["2001","Genesis mission launched to collect solar wind"],["2012","Voyager 1 crosses the heliopause"],["2018","Voyager 2 crosses the heliopause"],["2025","IMAP launched"]],
s2:[["Collecting the Sun","NASA’s Genesis spacecraft collected solar wind particles for over two years. Its capsule crashed in the Utah desert in 2004 when its parachutes failed, but scientists recovered many samples and measured the Sun’s composition."],["Solar sails","Spacecraft called solar sails are pushed mainly by the pressure of sunlight rather than the solar wind, but some proposed ‘electric sails’ would ride the solar wind’s charged particles."]],
sp:["Losing weight","Through the solar wind, the Sun loses about a million metric tons of material every second, but it is so massive that it has lost only a tiny fraction of its mass over billions of years."],
g:[["Solar wind","A stream of charged particles from the Sun"],["Heliosphere","The bubble inflated by the solar wind"],["Heliopause","The outer boundary of the heliosphere"],["Corona","The Sun’s outer atmosphere"]],
f:["The solar wind was predicted in 1958.","It usually travels about 400 km per second.","Voyager 1 left the heliosphere in 2012."],
fun:"The Sun loses about a million metric tons of material to the solar wind every second."});
